## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical containment design across IAM, network perimeters, key management, and rollback validation using synthetic tenants.

## Key Concepts
- IAM conditions for tenant isolation
- Perimeter controls: PSC/VC and VPC Service Controls
- CMEK rotation and data-key isolation
- Automated rollback with synthetic tenants and validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: conditional binding to isolate tenant access
const policy = { bindings: [] };
policy.bindings.push({
  role: "roles/storage.objectViewer",
  members: ["user:tenant-a@example.com"],
  condition: {
    title: "Tenant Access Restriction",
    description: "Restrict access to tenant-specific resources",
    expression: "resource.name.startsWith('projects/_/buckets/tenant-a-')"
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to apply concrete Google Cloud controls for multi-tenant isolation at runtime, beyond theoretical concepts, with a practical path from IAM to data and storage gates.

## Key Concepts

- IAM Conditions for per-tenant API access
- Storage prefixes and signed URLs for per-tenant data
- Per-tenant logging/alerting for exfiltration attempts
- CI gates that detect misconfig and roll back changes

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple tenant check in API middleware
function authorize(req, res, next) {
  const tenantFromToken = req.user?.tenantId;
  const requestedTenant = req.params.tenantId;
  if (tenantFromToken !== requestedTenant) return res.status(403).send('Forbidden');
  next();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the CI gate if a misconfig slips through?
- What are trade-offs of per-tenant prefixes vs separate buckets for storage isolation?
